    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born to Zapotec peasants, orphaned at three and speaking no Spanish until he was twelve, Juárez became a lawyer, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> and in 1858 president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</li><li>He led the Liberals to victory in the three-year Reform War and then led the Republic against the French invasion, until the Second Mexican Empire collapsed in 1867 and Maximilian was shot.</li><li>His long tenure, his emergency powers and his decision to run again in 1871 drew opposition from fellow Liberals, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>, who rebelled against him.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1858 Juárez became the first sitting Mexican ruler to leave the national territory, travelling by sea from Manzanillo through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-city">Panama City</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> to Veracruz, where he arrived on 4 May.</p></aside>
    </section>

